From: vincek@intergalact.com (Vince Kerchner)
Date: Sat, 10 Dec 1994 17:23:47 -0800
Subject: [#94-12-416] ECON: Book censorship?

This afternoon, I was at my local bookstore browsing, and had picked out a
few Greg Bear books, a copy of _Hot Spot_, and some other stuff, and
decided that I'd also like to get a copy of J. Neil Schulmans's _Stopping
Power_.  I looked in the new non-fiction, the social sciences, and the
policical section, and couldn't find it.  OK, no real problem.

However, while I was looking at their NY Times "best seller" rack, I
noticed that Wayne LaPierre's _Guns, Crime, and Freedom_ was on the list
(at #11 or something; in the middle), so I thought I'd get a copy of that
too.  Well, they seemed to have every other book on the list (including
_Bell Curve_), but not the LaPierre.  So, I thought, I'd better ask.  I
went to the info desk, had them call up the books on their database, and
they didn't have either of them.

They did offer to order the books for me, but I knew very well that other
bookstores down the street had them (I'd at least seen the Schulman), so I
declined.  However, after thinking about it for a little while, I took all
the other books I'd picked out and put them back on the racks, and said to
my spouse: "I'm done shopping here; I'd rather give my money to someone
else."

On the way home, we discussed the event, and I said something like: "I
would prefer to shop elsewhere from now on.  I think it's very strange that
they have all the other books on the best-seller list, but not the one by
Wayne LaPierre.  I think what they are doing is censorship."

Her reply was: "They should be able to stock and sell whatever books they
want to; what they're doing is not censorship.  No government is telling
them what to do."

I said: "I agree that they should be able to sell whatever they want, but I
do think that they're selectively choosing what to sell, based on topic and
content.  That's censorship, irrespective of whether the government forces
it on them or they choose it themselves.  I'd prefer to exercise my
free-market ability and spend my money elsewhere."

Now, this isn't a major issue or hardship for me, since there are at least
100 bookstores in a 10 mile radius of me, and I'll have no trouble at all
finding either of the books I want.  However, there are some issues I'd be
interested in feedback about:

  1. Assuming that a bookstore does selectively choose what to stock and
     sell based on content, is that censorship?  (The main reason I bring
     this up is that I was just browsing; I wouldn't even have known about
     the LaPierre book if I hadn't taken the time to read the best-seller
     list, but all the other books were on open display.)

  2. Assuming I disagree with the discrepancy between the market
     demographics (the best-seller list) and their choices, is the
     appropriate free-market response simply to go elsewhere?

--Vince

